damage: let the screen private outlive CloseScreen

Exiting the desktop session crashed Xphoenix-glamor-daemon every time.
damageCloseScreen() freed its screen private and then chained to the
downstream CloseScreen, but several layers keep calling into damage while
the server shuts down, and they do it after that point: composite's
compDestroyWindow, glamor's glamor_invalidate_stipple (via its GC
teardown), and misprite. Every damage entry point begins with
damageScrPriv(), and some then make an indirect call through
pScrPriv->funcs.*, so the freed allocation was dereferenced and in one
case jumped through.

Two narrower attempts did not converge, and are recorded here so they are
not retried:

  1. Deferring the free until after the downstream CloseScreen returns.
     Still a use-after-free -- the calls come from within that chain. It
     changed the symptom from a bad read (Data Abort at NULL+0x60) to a
     jump through the stale function pointer (PC alignment fault).
  2. Deferring the free, clearing the private slot so a later lookup
     returns NULL, and guarding DamageUnregister on it. That fixed
     DamageUnregister and moved the identical NULL dereference to
     compDestroyWindow.

There is no ordering that makes an early free safe, because damage does
not own the teardown order of the layers above it. So do not free it. It
is one struct per screen and the process exits moments later, which
reclaims it -- a bounded, one-shot leak traded for removing a whole class
of shutdown crash.

--- a/miext/damage/damage.c
+++ b/miext/damage/damage.c
@@ -1601,7 +1601,24 @@
     unwrap(pScrPriv, pScreen, CreateGC);
     unwrap(pScrPriv, pScreen, CopyWindow);
     unwrap(pScrPriv, pScreen, CloseScreen);
-    free(pScrPriv);
+
+    /* Do NOT free pScrPriv here, and do not clear its slot.
+     *
+     * Several layers keep calling into damage while the server shuts down, and
+     * they do it AFTER this runs: composite's compDestroyWindow, glamor's
+     * glamor_invalidate_stipple via its GC teardown, and misprite. Every damage
+     * entry point starts with damageScrPriv() and some then make an indirect call
+     * through pScrPriv->funcs.*, so freeing the private here is a use-after-free
+     * no matter where in this function the free sits.
+     *
+     * Guarding the callers one at a time was tried and does not converge: fixing
+     * DamageUnregister simply moved the same NULL dereference to
+     * compDestroyWindow. There is no ordering that makes an early free safe,
+     * because damage does not own the teardown order of the layers above it.
+     *
+     * So let the allocation outlive them. It is one struct per screen, and the
+     * process exits moments later, which reclaims it -- a bounded, one-shot leak
+     * traded for removing an entire class of shutdown crash. */
     return (*pScreen->CloseScreen) (pScreen);
 }
 
